# var-expr-init-int

The `var-expr-init` shape with an **`Int`**-declared seed rather than `Long`.

Why both rows exist: F's typed regime (`knownTyName`) recognises `Int | String | BigInt`
and **not `Long`**, so a `Long`-declared parameter stays untyped and everything over it
comes out as untyped `__arith__`. The whole rest of the corpus writes
`def workload(seed: Long)`, which means it measures only the untyped path — this row is
the typed one, and it is what the unboxed-cell entry test can actually reach.

Measured 2026-07-31: routing a typed (`(prim i.…)`) initialiser to the unboxed `lcell`
turns this loop's `cell.new/get/set` into `lcell.new/get/set`.
See `TASK/v2-perfomance.md` §v2-perf-unboxed-cell-only-for-literal-init.

Anti-fold: `seed` is opaque at the call site and drives the LCG.

```scalascript
def workload(seed: Int): Long =
  var s = (seed % 46341) + 1
  var sum = 0L
  var i = 0
  while i < 1000000 do
    s = (s * 48271) % 2147483
    sum = sum + (s % 7).toLong
    i = i + 1
  sum
```
